(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test13_1B"] = [{"id":"22105871","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 In a study of insect behavior, Samadi Galpayage and colleagues presented bumblebees with small wooden balls and observed many of the bees clinging to, rolling, and dragging the objects. The researchers provided no external rewards (such as food) to encourage these interactions. The bees simply appeared to be playing — and for no other reason than because they were having fun. Text 2 Insects do not have cortexes or other brain areas associated with emotions in humans. Still, Galpayage and her team have shown that bumblebees may engage in play, possibly experiencing some kind of positive emotional state. Other studies have suggested that bees experience negative emotional states (for example, stress), but as Galpayage and her team have acknowledged, emotions in insects, if they do indeed exist, are likely very rudimentary.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In a study of insect behavior, Samadi Galpayage and colleagues presented bumblebees with small wooden balls and observed many of the bees","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"clinging to, rolling, and dragging the objects. The researchers provided no external rewards (such as food) to encourage these interactions. The","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"bees simply appeared to be playing—and for no other reason than because they were having fun.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Insects do not have cortexes or other brain areas associated with emotions in humans. Still, Galpayage and her team have shown that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"bumblebees may engage in play, possibly experiencing some kind of positive emotional state. Other studies have suggested that bees","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"experience negative emotional states (for example, stress), but as Galpayage and her team have acknowledged, emotions in insects, if they do","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"indeed exist, are likely very rudimentary.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined portion of Text 1?","choices":["By objecting that the bees were actually experiencing a negative feeling akin to stress rather than a positive feeling","By arguing that some insects other than bumblebees may be capable of experiencing complex emotional states","By pointing out that even humans sometimes struggle to have fun while engaging in play","By noting that if the bees were truly playing, any positive feelings they may have experienced were probably quite basic"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["The","bees simply appeared to be playing—and for no other reason than because they were having fun."]},{"id":"b5898291","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The equipment from the Apollo Moon landings (1969 – 1972), such as radiation detectors and temperature probes, remains there to this day, but the data from these missions were mostly inaccessible until a recent data-transfer project made them ______. This project has allowed researcher Seiichi Nagihara to make use of the information in investigating temperature changes on the Moon.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The equipment from the Apollo Moon landings (1969–1972), such as radiation detectors and temperature probes, remains there to this day, but","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the data from these missions were mostly inaccessible until a recent data-transfer project made them ______. This project has allowed","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"researcher Seiichi Nagihara to make use of the information in investigating temperature changes on the Moon.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["predictable","complicated","representative","available"]},{"id":"c502943e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from Cynthia Kadohata’s 2004 novel Kira-Kira. [Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet. Even when he wasn’t talking, he made a lot of noise, clearing his throat and sniffing and tapping his fingers. ©2004 by Cynthia Kadohata","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Cynthia Kadohata’s 2004 novel Kira-Kira.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"[Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet. Even when he wasn’t talking, he made a lot of noise, clearing his throat and","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"sniffing and tapping his fingers.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"©2004 by Cynthia Kadohata","indent":4}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?","choices":["It lists the kinds of topics Uncle Katsuhisa enjoys discussing.","It suggests that Uncle Katsuhisa dislikes meeting new people.","It contrasts Uncle Katsuhisa with the narrator’s father.","It describes a conversation between the narrator and the narrator’s father."],"underline":"[Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet.","underline_segments":["[Uncle Katsuhisa] was as loud as my father was quiet."]},{"id":"975b0602","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"A number of Indigenous politicians have been elected to the United States Congress since 2000 as members of the country’s two established political parties. In Canada and several Latin American countries, on the other hand, Indigenous people have formed their own political parties to advance candidates who will advocate for the interests of their communities. This movement has been particularly successful in Ecuador, where Guadalupe Llori, a member of the Indigenous party known as Pachakutik, was elected president of the National Assembly in 2021.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A number of Indigenous politicians have been elected to the United States Congress since 2000 as members of the country’s two established","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"political parties. In Canada and several Latin American countries, on the other hand, Indigenous people have formed their own political parties to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"advance candidates who will advocate for the interests of their communities. This movement has been particularly successful in Ecuador, where","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Guadalupe Llori, a member of the Indigenous party known as Pachakutik, was elected president of the National Assembly in 2021.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To trace the history of an Indigenous political movement and speculate about its future development","To argue that Indigenous politicians in the United States should form their own political party","To highlight two approaches to achieving political representation for Indigenous people","To consider how Indigenous politicians in the United States have influenced Indigenous politicians in Canada and Latin America"]},{"id":"6bc0ba75","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The mimosa tree evolved in East Asia, where the beetle Bruchidius terrenus preys on its seeds. In 1785, mimosa trees were introduced to North America, far from any B. terrenus. But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents. Around 2001, B. terrenus was introduced in southeastern North America near where botanist Shu-Mei Chang and colleagues had been monitoring mimosa trees. Within a year, 93 percent of the trees had been attacked by the beetles.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The mimosa tree evolved in East Asia, where the beetle Bruchidius terrenus preys on its seeds. In 1785, mimosa trees were introduced to North","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"America, far from any B. terrenus. But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents. Around","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"2001, B. terrenus was introduced in southeastern North America near where botanist Shu-Mei Chang and colleagues had been monitoring","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"mimosa trees. Within a year, 93 percent of the trees had been attacked by the beetles.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It states the hypothesis that Chang and colleagues had set out to investigate using mimosa trees and B. terrenus.","It presents a generalization that is exemplified by the discussion of the mimosa trees and B. terrenus.","It provides context that clarifies why the species mentioned spread to new locations.","It offers an alternative explanation for the findings of Chang and colleagues."],"underline":"But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents.","underline_segments":["But evolutionary links between predators and their prey can persist across centuries and continents."]},{"id":"dc0bca21","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"It would be a mistake to ______ the exhibit that artist and curator Joe Baker, who is a member of the Lenape (Delaware) people, has organized at the Brooklyn Public Library. The exhibit, which includes Lenape beadwork from the 1850s as well as modern works that use traditional patterns, is essential viewing.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"It would be a mistake to ______ the exhibit that artist and curator Joe Baker, who is a member of the Lenape (Delaware) people, has organized at","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the Brooklyn Public Library. The exhibit, which includes Lenape beadwork from the 1850s as well as modern works that use traditional patterns,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"is essential viewing.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["complicate","amplify","overlook","assemble"]},{"id":"190857f0","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Why do sand cats purr but lions roar? Researchers hypothesize that this difference between the two feline species may be partly due to a U- shaped bone in their throats called the hyoid. Sand cats, which are much smaller than lions, have a rigid hyoid that rumbles when the cat’s larynx vibrates, resulting in a purr. By contrast, lions have a somewhat flexible hyoid, and the bone is attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that sand cats lack. These traits allow lions and most other species of big cats to produce powerful roars. The same traits may also prevent most big cats from purring.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Why do sand cats purr but lions roar? Researchers hypothesize that this difference between the two feline species may be partly due to a U-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"shaped bone in their throats called the hyoid. Sand cats, which are much smaller than lions, have a rigid hyoid that rumbles when the cat’s larynx","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"vibrates, resulting in a purr. By contrast, lions have a somewhat flexible hyoid, and the bone is attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sand cats lack. These traits allow lions and most other species of big cats to produce powerful roars. The same traits may also prevent most big","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cats from purring.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["The text presents a theory about two species, then discusses facts that weaken it.","The text compares the habitats of two species, then explains how those habitats are changing.","The text describes a behavior shared by two species, then discusses other behaviors shared by them.","The text poses a question about two species, then presents a possible answer."]},{"id":"f878693b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 In 1954 George Balanchine choreographed a production of The Nutcracker, a ballet by Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky. It has since become a tradition for hundreds of dance companies in North America to stage The Nutcracker each year. But the show is stuck in the past, with an old-fashioned story and references, so it should no longer be produced. Ballet needs to create new traditions if it wants to stay relevant to contemporary audiences. Text 2 The Nutcracker is outdated, but it should be kept because it’s a holiday favorite and provides substantial income for some dance companies. Although it can be behind the times, there are creative ways to update the show. For example, Debbie Allen successfully modernized the story. Her show Hot Chocolate Nutcracker combines ballet, tap, hip-hop, and other styles, and it has been gaining in popularity since it opened in 2009.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 1954 George Balanchine choreographed a production of The Nutcracker, a ballet by Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky. It has since become a tradition","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"for hundreds of dance companies in North America to stage The Nutcracker each year. But the show is stuck in the past, with an old-fashioned","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"story and references, so it should no longer be produced. Ballet needs to create new traditions if it wants to stay relevant to contemporary","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"audiences.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The Nutcracker is outdated, but it should be kept because it’s a holiday favorite and provides substantial income for some dance companies.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Although it can be behind the times, there are creative ways to update the show. For example, Debbie Allen successfully modernized the story.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Her show Hot Chocolate Nutcracker combines ballet, tap, hip-hop, and other styles, and it has been gaining in popularity since it opened in 2009.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?","choices":["By questioning the idea that the story of The Nutcracker is stuck in the past and by rejecting the suggestion that contemporary audiences would enjoy an updated version","By agreeing that contemporary audiences have largely stopped going to see performances of The Nutcracker because it’s so old-fashioned","By pointing out that most dance companies could increase their incomes by offering modernized versions of The Nutcracker","By suggesting that dance companies should consider offering revised versions of The Nutcracker instead of completely rejecting the show"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["But the show is stuck in the past, with an old-fashioned","story and references, so it should no longer be produced."]},{"id":"251e5281","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Studies of ocean wave breaking have predominantly focused on traveling waves (those propagating along the horizontal plane), so Mark McAllister et al. utilized a circular wave tank to produce and study spike waves, axisymmetric standing waves that can erupt vertically when traveling waves propagating in opposing directions intersect. Traveling waves break when wave steepness (height-to-length ratio) passes a critical threshold; breaking thus constrains wave height. McAllister et al. found that spike waves can exceed that constraint, as other factors than just steepness (e.g., jet stability and cavity shape) mediate spike-wave breaking.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Studies of ocean wave breaking have predominantly focused on traveling waves (those propagating along the horizontal plane), so Mark","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"McAllister et al. utilized a circular wave tank to produce and study spike waves, axisymmetric standing waves that can erupt vertically when","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"traveling waves propagating in opposing directions intersect. Traveling waves break when wave steepness (height-to-length ratio) passes a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"critical threshold; breaking thus constrains wave height. McAllister et al. found that spike waves can exceed that constraint, as other factors than","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"just steepness (e.g., jet stability and cavity shape) mediate spike-wave breaking.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Previous studies have suggested that steepness mediates breaking in traveling waves, but the study by McAllister et al. shows that jet stability and cavity shape may also influence breaking in such waves.","The process of breaking limits the height of traveling waves, but the study by McAllister et al. suggests that spike waves can exceed those limits if their height-to-length ratio reaches a critical threshold.","McAllister et al. suggest that spike waves can form when traveling waves propagating in opposing directions intersect and that spike waves tend to be higher than traveling waves.","The study by McAllister et al. suggests that when traveling waves intersect in specific ways, the resulting wave may be higher than would be expected based on the properties of traveling waves."]},{"id":"d1539546","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Tides can deposit large quantities of dead vegetation within a salt marsh, smothering healthy plants and leaving a salt panne — a depression devoid of plants that tends to trap standing water — in the marsh’s interior. Ecologist Kathryn Beheshti and colleagues found that burrowing crabs living within these pannes improve drainage by loosening the soil, leading the pannes to shrink as marsh plants move back in. At salt marsh edges, however, crab-induced soil loosening can promote marsh loss by accelerating erosion, suggesting that the burrowing action of crabs ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Tides can deposit large quantities of dead vegetation within a salt marsh, smothering healthy plants and leaving a salt panne—a depression","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"devoid of plants that tends to trap standing water—in the marsh’s interior. Ecologist Kathryn Beheshti and colleagues found that burrowing crabs","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"living within these pannes improve drainage by loosening the soil, leading the pannes to shrink as marsh plants move back in. At salt marsh","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"edges, however, crab-induced soil loosening can promote marsh loss by accelerating erosion, suggesting that the burrowing action of crabs","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["can be beneficial to marshes with small pannes but can be harmful to marshes with large pannes.","may promote increases in marsh plants or decreases in marsh plants, depending on the crabs location.","tends to be more heavily concentrated in areas of marsh interiors with standing water than at marsh edges.","varies in intensity depending on the size of the panne relative to the size of the surrounding marsh."]},{"id":"25290c8d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"“On Virtue” is a 1766 poem by Phillis Wheatley. Wheatley addresses the poem directly to the quality of virtue, imploring it to assist her in reaching a future goal: ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“On Virtue” is a 1766 poem by Phillis Wheatley. Wheatley addresses the poem directly to the quality of virtue, imploring it to assist her in reaching","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"a future goal: ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which quotation from “On Virtue” most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["“Attend me, Virtue, thro my youthful years! / O leave me not to the false joys of time! / But guide my steps to endless life and bliss.”","“I cease to wonder, and no more attempt / Thine height t’explore, or fathom thy profound.”","“O thou bright jewel in my aim I strive / To comprehend thee. Thine own words declare / Wisdom is higher than a fool can reach.”","“But, O my soul, sink not into despair, / Virtue is near thee, and with gentle hand / Would now embrace thee, hovers o er thine head.”"]},{"id":"f9bd4e61","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"German theater practitioner Bertolt Brecht (1898 – 1956) believed that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from audiences, provoking them to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage. Brecht’s influence can be seen in English playwright Caryl Churchill’s 1979 play Cloud 9: although the play sometimes invites empathetic reactions, it primarily works to engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism, which it does by placing audiences at a distance, thereby encouraging them to ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"German theater practitioner Bertolt Brecht (1898–1956) believed that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"audiences, provoking them to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage. Brecht’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"influence can be seen in English playwright Caryl Churchill’s 1979 play Cloud 9: although the play sometimes invites empathetic reactions, it","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"primarily works to engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism, which it does by placing audiences at a distance, thereby","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"encouraging them to ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["focus on the characters beliefs about social and political issues as revealed by the characters actions.","reflect on social and political phenomena not directly related to patriarchy and colonialism.","recognize pertinent social and political parallels between Germany during Brecht’s time and England at the time when Churchill was writing Cloud 9.","be dispassionate as they think critically about the social and political questions raised by the play."]},{"id":"2edd7ffe","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Archaeologist Veronica Waweru visited the Lewa Wildlife Conservancy in Kenya. While exploring there, Waweru noticed many rows of shallow pits carved into stone. Waweru believed the pits could have been used as game boards to play ancient versions of mancala, a two-person strategy game that is still popular today. Some of the more recent pits were carved on top of older pits. This led Waweru to think that the game was played at the site for a long time.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Archaeologist Veronica Waweru visited the Lewa Wildlife Conservancy in Kenya. While exploring there, Waweru noticed many rows of shallow","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pits carved into stone. Waweru believed the pits could have been used as game boards to play ancient versions of mancala, a two-person","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"strategy game that is still popular today. Some of the more recent pits were carved on top of older pits. This led Waweru to think that the game","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was played at the site for a long time.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, why does Waweru think that mancala was played at the site for a long time?","choices":["Some of the newer pits were carved on top of the older pits.","She noticed many rows of shallow pits carved into stone.","She discovered the pits at the Lewa Wildlife Conservancy.","Some of the pits were shallow and others were quite deep."]},{"id":"28dfc05a","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Even with the widespread adoption of personal computers, many authors still choose to write and revise their novels by hand and only then transcribe the final version on a computer. It may be tempting to speculate about how a novel written this way would be affected if it had been exclusively typed instead, but each novel is a unique entity resulting from a specific set of circumstances. Therefore, ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Even with the widespread adoption of personal computers, many authors still choose to write and revise their novels by hand and only then","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"transcribe the final version on a computer. It may be tempting to speculate about how a novel written this way would be affected if it had been","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"exclusively typed instead, but each novel is a unique entity resulting from a specific set of circumstances. Therefore, ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["in order to increase their efficiency, authors who currently write their novels largely by hand should instead work only on a computer.","authors who do most of their drafting and revising by hand likely have more success than those who work entirely on a computer.","novels written by hand take less time to produce, on average, than novels written on a computer do.","there is no way to reasonably evaluate how a work would be different if it had been written by other means."]},{"id":"668f75cb","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is from Ahmet Hamdi Tanpinar’s 1961 novel The Time Regulation Institute (translated from the Turkish in 2014 by Maureen Freely and Alexander Dawe). The narrator was once involved with the Time Regulation Institute, a fictional bureaucracy that regulates the time of Turkey’s clocks. I may be the most humble and absurd man in the world and, as my wife says, the most slovenly creature you may ever meet — that — is, before the founding of our institute but I did come to know a truly great man who possessed a natural genius for invention. I spent years at his side. I watched the way he worked. I witnessed how an idea would suddenly catch fire in his mind and take shape, like a tree sprouting shoots and branches, before coming into being. ©2014 by Maureen Freely and Alexander Dawe","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Ahmet Hamdi Tanpinar’s 1961 novel The Time Regulation Institute (translated from the Turkish in 2014 by Maureen","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Freely and Alexander Dawe). The narrator was once involved with the Time Regulation Institute, a fictional bureaucracy that regulates the time of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Turkey’s clocks.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I may be the most humble and absurd man in the world and, as my wife says, the most slovenly creature you may ever meet—that","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"is, before the founding of our institute—but I did come to know a truly great man who possessed a natural genius for invention. I","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"spent years at his side. I watched the way he worked. I witnessed how an idea would suddenly catch fire in his mind and take","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"shape, like a tree sprouting shoots and branches, before coming into being.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"©2014 by Maureen Freely and Alexander Dawe","indent":4}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A person was fortunate enough to have found love despite his obvious shortcomings.","An exceptionally talented person professes humility to avoid intimidating others.","A person appreciates the extensive time he passed in the company of someone he deeply admired.","A truly brilliant person lets ideas mature in his mind before expressing them aloud."]},{"id":"f868d438","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In 2018, the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek ______ were featured in City Dreams, a solo exhibition at New York’s Museum of Modern Art.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 2018, the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek ______ were featured in City Dreams, a solo exhibition at New York’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Museum of Modern Art.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Kingelez;","Kingelez,","Kingelez","Kingelez:"]},{"id":"be34a3df","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In 2008, two years after the death of science fiction writer Octavia Butler, the Huntington Library in ______ received a collection of more than 8,000 items, including Butler’s private notes, research materials, manuscripts, photos, and drawings. Today, the Octavia E. Butler Collection is one of the most researched archives at the library.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 2008, two years after the death of science fiction writer Octavia Butler, the Huntington Library in ______ received a collection of more than","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"8,000 items, including Butler’s private notes, research materials, manuscripts, photos, and drawings. Today, the Octavia E. Butler Collection is one","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of the most researched archives at the library.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["California,","California:","California —","California"]},{"id":"603755a5","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In 1959, marine biologist Dr. Albert Jones founded the Underwater Adventure Seekers, a scuba diving ______ that is the oldest club for Black divers in the United States and that has helped thousands of diving enthusiasts become certified in the field.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1959, marine biologist Dr. Albert Jones founded the Underwater Adventure Seekers, a scuba diving ______ that is the oldest club for Black","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"divers in the United States and that has helped thousands of diving enthusiasts become certified in the field.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["club","club,","club —","club, and"]},{"id":"84658166","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In 1943, in the midst of World War II, mathematics professor Grace Hopper was recruited by the US military to help the war effort by solving complex equations. Hopper’s subsequent career would involve more than just ______ as a pioneering computer programmer, Hopper would help usher in the digital age.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1943, in the midst of World War II, mathematics professor Grace Hopper was recruited by the US military to help the war effort by solving","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"complex equations. Hopper’s subsequent career would involve more than just ______ as a pioneering computer programmer, Hopper would help","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"usher in the digital age.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["equations, though:","equations, though,","equations. Though,","equations though"]},{"id":"a5079e0d","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Horsepower is a unit of measurement used to determine how much power a vehicle produces. The measurement is based on how much and how quickly weight can be ______ one unit of mechanical horsepower is equivalent to the amount of power it takes to lift 550 pounds one foot off the ground in one second.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Horsepower is a unit of measurement used to determine how much power a vehicle produces. The measurement is based on how much and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"how quickly weight can be ______ one unit of mechanical horsepower is equivalent to the amount of power it takes to lift 550 pounds one foot off","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the ground in one second.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["moved, for example,","moved,","moved; for example,","moved"]},{"id":"04bfd364","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"The intense pressure found in the deep ocean can affect the structure of proteins in fish’s cells, distorting the proteins’ shape. The chemical trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO) counters this effect, ensuring that proteins retain their original ______ is found in high concentrations in the cells of the deepest-dwelling fish.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The intense pressure found in the deep ocean can affect the structure of proteins in fish’s cells, distorting the proteins’ shape. The chemical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO) counters this effect, ensuring that proteins retain their original ______ is found in high concentrations in the cells","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of the deepest-dwelling fish.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["configurations. TMAO","configurations TMAO","configurations, TMAO","configurations and TMAO"]},{"id":"9c78f702","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Phytoplankton play a crucial role in the ocean’s uptake of carbon from the atmosphere. When alive, these tiny marine organisms absorb atmospheric carbon via photosynthesis. ______ after they die, the phytoplankton sink to the seafloor, where the carbon in their cells gets stored in sediment, preventing it from cycling back into the atmosphere.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Phytoplankton play a crucial role in the ocean’s uptake of carbon from the atmosphere. When alive, these tiny marine organisms absorb","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"atmospheric carbon via photosynthesis. ______ after they die, the phytoplankton sink to the seafloor, where the carbon in their cells gets stored in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sediment, preventing it from cycling back into the atmosphere.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Specifically,","By contrast,","Nevertheless,","Then,"]},{"id":"b0620764","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Phobetor, a name drawn from Greek mythology, is an exoplanet that orbits the star PSR B1257+12, also known as Lich. Phobetor’s mass is 0.01 times that of Jupiter, or 0.01 Jupiter masses. Mastika, which means “gem” or “jewel” in Malay, is an exoplanet that orbits the star HD 179949, also known as Gumala. Mastika’s mass is 0.92 Jupiter masses. The student wants to make and support a generalization about exoplanets.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Phobetor, a name drawn from Greek mythology, is an exoplanet that orbits the star PSR B1257+12, also known as Lich.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Phobetor’s mass is 0.01 times that of Jupiter, or 0.01 Jupiter masses.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Mastika, which means “gem” or “jewel” in Malay, is an exoplanet that orbits the star HD 179949, also known as Gumala.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Mastika’s mass is 0.92 Jupiter masses.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Exoplanets that are named Phobetor orbit Lich, and those that are named Mastika orbit Gumala.","Even though Phobetor and Mastika are both exoplanets, their masses are different: Phobetor’s mass is 0.01 Jupiter masses, and Mastika’s is 0.92 Jupiter masses.","Many stars have both a designation and a proper name; for instance, PSR B1257+12 is also known as Lich, and HD 179949 is also known as Gumala.","Exoplanet names have diverse origins, a fact that can be seen in the cases of Phobetor, a name drawn from Greek mythology, and Mastika, which means “gem” or “jewel” in Malay."]},{"id":"74028acf","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Topographic maps show the elevation of landforms above sea level. Bathymetric maps, ______ show the elevation of landforms below the sea, providing valuable information to marine geophysicists like Claudia Flores, who studies seismic data from the northeastern Caribbean.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Topographic maps show the elevation of landforms above sea level. Bathymetric maps, ______ show the elevation of landforms below the sea,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"providing valuable information to marine geophysicists like Claudia Flores, who studies seismic data from the northeastern Caribbean.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["in conclusion,","for example,","by contrast,","afterward,"]},{"id":"332e75bf","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In Annie Dillard’s Pilgrim at Tinker Creek — where, early on, the author marvels at a single goldfish’s delicate fins but later winces when imagining a horde of goldfish laying and eating their own eggs — Dillard struggles to reconcile the complicated juxtapositions of the natural world. ______ nature’s mesmerizing intricacy and pitiless harshness prove inextricably linked for Dillard, like “two branches of the same creek.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In Annie Dillard’s Pilgrim at Tinker Creek—where, early on, the author marvels at a single goldfish’s delicate fins but later winces when imagining a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"horde of goldfish laying and eating their own eggs—Dillard struggles to reconcile the complicated juxtapositions of the natural world. ______","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"nature’s mesmerizing intricacy and pitiless harshness prove inextricably linked for Dillard, like “two branches of the same creek.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["To that end,","Ultimately,","Moreover,","Hence,"]},{"id":"25adba4e","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 1897, twenty Black US Army infantrymen rode bicycles from Montana to Missouri. The 1,900-mile journey took forty-one days. The goal was to test the idea of forming a military bicycle corps. In 2022, Erick Cede ño, a Black long-distance cyclist, reenacted the journey. Cede ño wanted to honor the infantrymen on the journey’s 125th anniversary. The student wants to emphasize how far the infantrymen traveled.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 1897, twenty Black US Army infantrymen rode bicycles from Montana to Missouri.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The 1,900-mile journey took forty-one days.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The goal was to test the idea of forming a military bicycle corps.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In 2022, Erick Cedeño, a Black long-distance cyclist, reenacted the journey.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Cedeño wanted to honor the infantrymen on the journey’s 125th anniversary.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The US infantrymen rode their bicycles from Montana to Missouri — traveling a total of 1,900 miles.","The 125th anniversary of the infantrymen’s journey was in 2022.","The goal of the 1897 journey was to test the idea of forming a military bicycle corps.","Over a century later, Erick Cede ño honored the infantrymen by reenacting their 1897 journey."]},{"id":"964c6055","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Two opposing theories of vision divided scholars for many centuries. The ancient Greek mathematician Euclid (circa 300 BCE) supported the extramission theory. This theory held that the eyes emit a form of radiation that illuminates objects in its range. The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle (384 –322 BCE) supported the intromission theory. This theory held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes. In the eleventh century, Arab mathematician Ibn al-Haytham (965 –1040 CE) largely settled the debate with the first conclusive experiments supporting intromission. The student wants to provide a historical overview of the two theories.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Two opposing theories of vision divided scholars for many centuries.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The ancient Greek mathematician Euclid (circa 300 BCE) supported the extramission theory.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"This theory held that the eyes emit a form of radiation that illuminates objects in its range.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle (384–322 BCE) supported the intromission theory.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This theory held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the eleventh century, Arab mathematician Ibn al-Haytham (965–1040 CE) largely settled the debate with the first conclusive","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"experiments supporting intromission.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Scholars were divided between the extramission and intromission theories of vision until Ibn al-Haytham’s eleventh-century experiments largely settled the debate in support of intromission.","Through two opposing theories of vision —extramission and intromission — Euclid, Aristotle, and Ibn al-Haytham held that a form of radiation is emitted either from objects or from the eyes.","While Ibn al-Haytham largely settled the debate in the eleventh century, Aristotle supported the theory of intromission centuries before.","Before the eleventh century, the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle supported the intromission theory, which held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes."]}];
